{"name":"Palace Green","hex":"#426255","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"palace","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Official residence of a head of state or other dignitary, especially in a monarchical or imperial governmental system.","A large and lavishly ornate residence."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To decorate or ornate."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/palace"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Palace Green","blocks":[{"text":"Palace Green is an open space in the centre of Durham, England, flanked by Durham Cathedral and Durham Castle. The Cathedral and Castle together form a UNESCO World Heritage Site."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/Durham_Cathedral_from_Palace_Green.jpg/330px-Durham_Cathedral_from_Palace_Green.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palace_Green","wikidata":"Q7126168"},{"title":"Palace","blocks":[{"text":"A palace is a large residence, often serving as a royal residence or the home for a head of state or another high-ranking dignitary, such as a bishop or archbishop. The word is derived from the Latin word palātium, for Palatine Hill in Rome, which housed the Imperial residences."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/Palacio_Real_de_Madrid_Julio_2016_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Palacio_Real_de_Madrid_Julio_2016_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palace","wikidata":"Q16560"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Durham_-_Palace_Green.jpg/500px-Durham_-_Palace_Green.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Durham_-_Palace_Green.jpg/1280px-Durham_-_Palace_Green.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Durham_-_Palace_Green.jpg/500px-Durham_-_Palace_Green.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Students resting on Palace Green","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADurham_-_Palace_Green.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/Palace_Green_Durham_